// Cross-observation: the two HYG files disagree on llvm_emit_call.oo
// (255 vs 247) and llvm_emit_fn.oo (253 vs 255). One of them is wrong
// about each. "Fresh" is not authoritative just because it is newer;
// the on-disk read is the only authority. plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot
// already proves this: it gets match_expr right (256) but is off by
// +11 on expr (claims 242, actual 253).

// ============================================================
// 3. The HYG principle
// ============================================================
//
// A HYG file freezes line counts at write time. The line lock is a
// static invariant about the source tree; the source tree is dynamic.
// So a HYG file written on day D is stale on day D+1 for any file
// that got edited in between. This is not a bug; it is the pattern.
//
// Two failure modes follow:
//
//   (i)   Treat HYG as live. A reader acts on the stale number
//         (e.g. assumes 227 lines of headroom for match_expr when
//         actual is 256 — zero headroom, file is at the ceiling).
//
//   (ii)  Write HYG once, never regenerate. The next HYG writer
//         does not know the old one exists, writes a divergent one,
//         and the project ends up with two HYG files that disagree.
//
// The first HYG in this lane (plan_llvm_hyg.oot) is in (i) plus (ii):
// a newer HYG (plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot) was written, the older was
// not marked residual, and downstream readers have to know which to
// trust. The expr row shows (ii) inside (ii): even the "fresh" HYG
// is 11 lines off the real number.

// ============================================================
// 4. Recommendation
// ============================================================
//
// Pick one:
//   (a) Mark plan_llvm_hyg.oot residual; plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot is
//       the single source of truth.
//   (b) Hand-update plan_llvm_hyg.oot to current numbers.
//   (c) Auto-regenerate plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot from disk via a
//       process script.
//
// Recommended: (a) + (c). The stale file becomes a frozen historical
// artifact, the fresh file is regenerated from disk.
//
// Reject (b): hand-updating recreates the same rot on the next edit
// and gives a false sense of currency.
//
// Reject (c) alone: the stale file will keep being cited as if it
// were live, and readers will not know to ignore it.

// ============================================================
// 5. Proposed fix (concrete text)
// ============================================================
//
// 5.1  Prepend a residual header to plan_llvm_hyg.oot. Do NOT delete
//      the file; the historical counts are evidence and audits may
//      still want to cite them.
//
//      Text to insert at the top of plan_llvm_hyg.oot (before
//      line 1, which is currently "# LLVM HYG — line lock"):
//
//        # RESIDUAL 2026-08-19
//        # This file is frozen. Source of truth is
//        #   openOODA/audit/plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot
//        # Drift report: openOODA/audit/hyg_drift_2026_08_19.oot
//        # Do not cite numbers from this file; they are stale.
//
// 5.2  Add a one-line generated-by note to plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot so
//      future readers know it is regenerable:
//
//        # Generated from ooda/oodac/llvm_emit_*.oo on 2026-08-19
//        # by openOODA/scripts/regen_llvm_hyg.oo
//        # Re-run after any emit edit; exit code is non-zero if any
//        # file is over 256 lines.
//
// 5.3  Add a new process tool, openOODA/scripts/regen_llvm_hyg.oo.
//      This is the workhorse that keeps (c) honest. Behavior:
//
//        1. list_dir("ooda/oodac/llvm_emit_*.oo") and the open68_*
//           smoke files referenced by plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot:8-33.
//        2. For each, open + iterate, counting newline characters.
//           Report the count as an Int (line count).
//        3. Sort by filename; write
//           openOODA/audit/plan_llvm_resi_hyg.oot with the count
//           list, preserving the existing 2-space column format.
//        4. Print a "near ceiling" warning for any file at >= 250
//           lines (matches the near-ceiling rows the HYG already
//           tracks).
//        5. Exit non-zero if any file is > 256 lines, so a future
//           CI step can block the rot before it lands.
//
// 5.4  Wire regen_llvm_hyg.oo into the verify_all.oo chain so the
//      HYG cannot drift silently between audit passes. Cite this
//      report from WORKFLOW.oot so the rule is discoverable.

// ============================================================
